2026-11-06T14:30:11.4221390Z 
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4221840Z Using `clang`, `clang++`, etc., requires a CLT installation at `/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools`.
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4227850Z If you don't want to install the CLT, you can write appropriate configuration files pointing to your
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4228570Z SDK at ~/.config/clang.
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4228790Z 
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4228950Z To use the bundled libunwind please use the following LDFLAGS:
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4229560Z   LDFLAGS="-L/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/lib/unwind -lunwind"
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4229780Z 
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4230010Z To use the bundled libc++ please use the following LDFLAGS:
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4230860Z   LDFLAGS="-L/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/lib/c++ -L/opt/homebrew/opt/llvm/lib/unwind -lunwind"
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4231430Z Features newer than system libc++ will require the following define to enable
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4232050Z (support for this may be removed in a future major LLVM release):
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4232480Z   CPPFLAGS="-D_LIBCPP_DISABLE_AVAILABILITY"
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4232720Z 
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4232970Z NOTE: You probably want to use the libunwind and libc++ provided by macOS unless you know what you're doing.
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4233410Z 
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4233650Z llvm is keg-only, which means it was not symlinked into /opt/homebrew,
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4234250Z because macOS already provides this software and installing another version in
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4234760Z parallel can cause all kinds of trouble.
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4235000Z 
2026-11-06T14:30:11.4235150Z If you need to have llvm first in your PATH, run:
